{"connection_id": "BC-bcd2896411e09fdc833e", "ref": "Didache 1:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways, life and death", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the path of those favored by God from the paths of wrath and straying; the Didache opens its instruction with two ways, one of life and one of death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72de0e0dc2e3b19c3827", "ref": "Didache 7:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "baptism in living water", "note": "The Didache prefers baptism in living water. Its ritual preference turns running or fresh water into a sign of new life, extending the commentary's literal contrast between a life-giving source and lost water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1cae7b77817fdfa8502", "ref": "Epistle of Barnabas 18:1–20:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way of light and way of darkness", "note": "The commentary asks for the straight path rather than the paths of wrath and straying; Barnabas sets out a way of light against a way of darkness, a Christian ethical expansion of the two-way contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e98b6aaa459aef6ad4c", "ref": "Origen, Homilies on Genesis 13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaac's wells read as scripture", "note": "Origen's homily on Isaac's reopened wells treats digging wells as recovering the deep waters of Scripture. The reading gives a Christian interpretive afterlife to the commentary's well as a source that must be reached and kept open."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4db385a10b85d2c2408", "ref": "Peshitta, John 3:16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Syriac ʿālmā, world", "note": "The commentary labels al-ʿālamīn from ʿ-L-M and also develops a deep, water-filled well from that family; the Peshitta uses cognate ʿālmā for “world,” showing the word’s spatial world sense in a Christian text."}
{"connection_id": "BC-612373a59d6933225b30", "ref": "Protevangelium of James 11:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Mary goes to draw water", "note": "In the usual chapter division, Mary goes out with a water jar and hears the annunciatory greeting. This Christian apocryphal well-side encounter belongs beside the commentary's women at water, where drawing water becomes a setting for divine gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5807b09f90e00dee4729", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud over the wilderness journey", "note": "The commentary brings cloud, travel, and water together; Paul recalls Israel’s ancestors under the cloud and on their wilderness passage, supplying the Christian pilgrimage setting for his water reference."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2507e0f30312ec6be421", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud over a travelling people", "note": "Israel's wilderness journey takes place under the cloud. Together with the water in verse 4, this creates a Christian rereading in which cloud and water accompany a people on the way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4d432ec6a8c2d581fcf", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Paul’s spiritual rock and drink", "note": "The commentary treats water as the literal support of the traveler; Paul rereads Israel’s wilderness drink from the rock as spiritual drink from Christ, adding a Christian interpretation to water on the journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c9d7502ce43eee0ba565", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water from the rock on the way", "note": "The commentary says water keeps a traveler’s work standing; Paul recalls Israel drinking from a spiritual rock in the wilderness, making provision on a journey the basis for a Christian reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5108fbb7ee593b0e8cc0", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water from the rock for the journey", "note": "The wilderness people drink from the spiritual rock that follows them. It is a strong counterpart to the commentary's claim that water beside the traveller sustains the journey and its affairs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3e2d57b1bfac616e6c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seed must die to live", "note": "The commentary connects rain-soaked soil, plant growth, and the raising of the dead; Paul says the seed is not made alive unless it dies, using planted growth to explain resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b890f438ece81416dd4e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.37", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a bare kernel becomes a plant", "note": "The commentary says rain leaves a green trace on earth; Paul distinguishes the bare kernel sown from the body that will grow, making plant emergence a model for resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16647d7f2ac611950d04", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "what is sown is raised", "note": "The commentary links renewed earth to the resurrection of the dead; Paul explicitly compares a perishable seed sown in the ground with the imperishable body raised."}
{"connection_id": "BC-278927ea85e54d5aaee3", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "planting, watering, divine growth", "note": "Paul plants, Apollos waters, and God gives the growth. Its agricultural distinction makes explicit the commentary's basic figure: water serves growth, yet the giver of growth remains God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3eba1861182895d65296", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers and flower falls", "note": "Human flesh is compared to grass that withers and a flower that falls. As an explicit inverse, it helps define the significance of the commentary's exceptional plant that does not wither in summer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7d806f36d24bdef6a7d", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "springs without water and driven mist", "note": "The false teachers are compared to waterless springs and mists driven by storm. Its paired failures of well and cloud form a precise inverse to the commentary's full well and productive cloud."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e09b6c224e850a22c4a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.14.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain, fruitful seasons, food", "note": "God gives rain from heaven and fruitful seasons, filling people with food and gladness. It exactly connects divine rain with the food and livelihood results developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8df531ea26848a111a70", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.31", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a fixed day to judge the world", "note": "The commentary names the Day of Judgment; Paul says God appointed a day to judge the inhabited world in righteousness and gave assurance by raising the appointed man."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4f87d1c4678c95b7fe9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.8.36", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water encountered on a road", "note": "On a wilderness road the Ethiopian traveller sees water and asks for baptism. The scene is a Christian travel-and-water meeting that gives the commentary's road beside water a ritual extension."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d1c6fa925032f97ed10", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "land drinks rain and yields plants", "note": "Land that drinks repeatedly falling rain and produces useful vegetation receives blessing. It directly restates the commentary's heavenly water making the earth grow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-875aecd51b4a936abe95", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farmer waits for early and late rain", "note": "The farmer waits for the earth's precious fruit through the early and late rains. It offers a New Testament seasonal analogue to the commentary's first rain and rain-grown vegetation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc65dcf4ea769e9e8436", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "John repeats Isaiah’s straight-way formula", "note": "The commentary asks to be guided on the straight path; John the Baptist identifies himself with Isaiah’s voice calling for the Lord’s way to be made straight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b7fd10435d577cf9dc4", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.38", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic rabbi, teacher or master", "note": "The commentary calls the plant-growing cloud by the Qur’anic title Rabb; John preserves the Aramaic address rabbi and glosses it as teacher, showing a related Semitic master-title in a human teaching relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b86a8fbd78e47ff3e69", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ordinary well water becomes living water", "note": "The commentary insists that a traveler’s water sustains his affairs; Jesus interprets the well encounter through the gift of living water, adding a spiritual reading to literal thirst."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e309d150272d4da63125", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gift of God and living water", "note": "Jesus offers living water at the well. It moves the commentary's full, beneficial well from physical support to a divine gift without erasing the scene of drawing water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8725d12b1bc7dff6fd3a", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water becomes an inner spring", "note": "The commentary follows rain and well water into enduring life; Jesus says the water he gives becomes a spring within the recipient, a Christian expansion from provision to lasting life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ecc5655c309c5ee4dce", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water becomes an inner spring", "note": "The water Jesus gives becomes a spring welling up to eternal life. Its self-renewing spring is a concentrated Christian analogue to the commentary's water that keeps life and affairs standing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de75b9cf67ab79330aed", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weary traveler rests at a well", "note": "The commentary’s Musa reaches a well after travel and later withdraws to shade; Jesus, tired from his journey, sits beside Jacob’s well, a close Christian well-side travel scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87de0bd35529de24dfd2", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "weary traveller seated at Jacob's well", "note": "Jesus, wearied by travel, sits at Jacob's well. This makes a close well-side counterpart to the commentary's traveller who reaches water and whose journey depends on it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccd841b12644758bb092", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "request for water at the well", "note": "The commentary has Musa water women at Midian; Jesus asks a Samaritan woman for a drink at Jacob’s well, making the well encounter turn on who gives water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd132f3e4dc466e6a694", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "woman comes to draw water", "note": "A woman arrives to draw water, turning the well into a meeting between a traveller and a woman obtaining water. It closely accompanies the commentary's Musa, the two women, and the Midian water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2280e58f25ea5fb5d1e7", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water inaccessible without human help", "note": "The sick man says that when the pool's water is stirred, he has no one to put him in it before another enters. It supplies a Christian scene where need, water, and the absence of a helper meet, a useful counterpart to the women who cannot water without aid."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d344674af96004991b3", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.37", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thirsty people are invited to come", "note": "The commentary says water keeps the traveler from thirst and supports life; Jesus invites the thirsty to come and drink, turning thirst into a direct spiritual appeal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f287175ea6d2287f25fa", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.38", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "living water flows out", "note": "The commentary traces water from source to soil and growth; Jesus describes rivers of living water flowing from the believer, extending the image from provision to inward abundance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bafa92e23609341baf8a", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.38", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "streams of living water", "note": "The believer is described as the source from whom streams of living water flow. It extends the living-water image from the well to an overflowing source, a useful counterpart to the commentary's abundant gathered water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80835ecfdd38a88e828b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jude.1.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "waterless clouds driven by winds", "note": "Jude's false teachers are waterless clouds carried by winds. This is a deliberate negative inversion of the commentary's moist, rain-bearing cloud that grows plants: a cloud that promises but gives no life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a9550617d3b6080b74d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.79", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feet guided into the way of peace", "note": "The commentary asks God to guide “us” on a straight path; Zechariah’s hymn says the dawn from on high will guide people’s feet into the way of peace."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8f3f2dfb562824e1bf3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "name and kingdom in prayer", "note": "The commentary moves from invoking God’s name to divine rule and guidance; Luke’s Lord’s Prayer likewise begins with the Father’s name and kingdom before its requests."}

