{"connection_id": "BC-8725d12b1bc7dff6fd3a", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water becomes an inner spring", "note": "The commentary follows rain and well water into enduring life; Jesus says the water he gives becomes a spring within the recipient, a Christian expansion from provision to lasting life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ecc5655c309c5ee4dce", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water becomes an inner spring", "note": "The water Jesus gives becomes a spring welling up to eternal life. Its self-renewing spring is a concentrated Christian analogue to the commentary's water that keeps life and affairs standing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de75b9cf67ab79330aed", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weary traveler rests at a well", "note": "The commentary’s Musa reaches a well after travel and later withdraws to shade; Jesus, tired from his journey, sits beside Jacob’s well, a close Christian well-side travel scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87de0bd35529de24dfd2", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "weary traveller seated at Jacob's well", "note": "Jesus, wearied by travel, sits at Jacob's well. This makes a close well-side counterpart to the commentary's traveller who reaches water and whose journey depends on it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccd841b12644758bb092", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "request for water at the well", "note": "The commentary has Musa water women at Midian; Jesus asks a Samaritan woman for a drink at Jacob’s well, making the well encounter turn on who gives water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd132f3e4dc466e6a694", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "woman comes to draw water", "note": "A woman arrives to draw water, turning the well into a meeting between a traveller and a woman obtaining water. It closely accompanies the commentary's Musa, the two women, and the Midian water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2280e58f25ea5fb5d1e7", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water inaccessible without human help", "note": "The sick man says that when the pool's water is stirred, he has no one to put him in it before another enters. It supplies a Christian scene where need, water, and the absence of a helper meet, a useful counterpart to the women who cannot water without aid."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d344674af96004991b3", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.37", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thirsty people are invited to come", "note": "The commentary says water keeps the traveler from thirst and supports life; Jesus invites the thirsty to come and drink, turning thirst into a direct spiritual appeal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f287175ea6d2287f25fa", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.38", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "living water flows out", "note": "The commentary traces water from source to soil and growth; Jesus describes rivers of living water flowing from the believer, extending the image from provision to inward abundance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bafa92e23609341baf8a", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.38", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "streams of living water", "note": "The believer is described as the source from whom streams of living water flow. It extends the living-water image from the well to an overflowing source, a useful counterpart to the commentary's abundant gathered water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80835ecfdd38a88e828b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jude.1.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "waterless clouds driven by winds", "note": "Jude's false teachers are waterless clouds carried by winds. This is a deliberate negative inversion of the commentary's moist, rain-bearing cloud that grows plants: a cloud that promises but gives no life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a9550617d3b6080b74d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.79", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feet guided into the way of peace", "note": "The commentary asks God to guide “us” on a straight path; Zechariah’s hymn says the dawn from on high will guide people’s feet into the way of peace."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8f3f2dfb562824e1bf3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "name and kingdom in prayer", "note": "The commentary moves from invoking God’s name to divine rule and guidance; Luke’s Lord’s Prayer likewise begins with the Father’s name and kingdom before its requests."}

