{"connection_id": "BC-1404b1085a2b4c7ffa47", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "few find the life-giving way", "note": "The commentary asks to be guided on the straight road; Jesus says the way to life is narrow and difficult and few find it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-90c49170ca9c14d26a9d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.11.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shut sky so rain does not fall", "note": "The witnesses have authority to shut the sky so that rain does not fall during their prophecy. Its withheld-rain image is a specific inverse to the commentary's cloud and rain as mercy that restores life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05f2254739f59778b851", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "books opened for judgment", "note": "The commentary names the Day of Judgment; Revelation depicts the dead standing before the throne and being judged from the opened books according to their deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4d9b25d0f59faaf7dbf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.21.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water of life given freely", "note": "The thirsty receive water of life freely from the spring. It gives an eschatological form to the commentary's water as indispensable gift and remedy for need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0079ada1e8bd9472db55", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "river flows from the throne", "note": "The commentary calls water the support and “king” of human affairs; Revelation’s river of life flows from the throne of God and the Lamb, joining rule to life-giving water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59d29482ed5b4197c542", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "river of water of life", "note": "A bright river of water of life proceeds from God's throne. The throne-source relationship gives a Christian counterpart to the commentary's unusual claim that water is a kingdom's or traveller's holding power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c200fdae91dbc30ce7ab", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree and water sustain life", "note": "The commentary links gathered water to persistent vegetation; Revelation places the tree of life beside the river, bearing fruit and leaves for healing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db2b429835b1d8509289", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruit trees and healing leaves", "note": "The trees beside the life-river bear fruit continually and their leaves serve healing. Its flourishing trees supply a Christian counterpart to the commentary's plant nourished into unfading freshness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0e5759ebc635fdd0199", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide to springs of living water", "note": "The commentary joins guidance on a road to water for the traveler; the Lamb shepherds the redeemed and guides them to springs of living water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c7910c470a5270054fc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guidance to springs of life-water", "note": "The Lamb shepherds and guides the thirsty to springs of the water of life. It closely meets the commentary's way, guidance, shepherding provision, and water that removes want."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a63fb5a1aba4cfa5dec5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seal protects servants before harm", "note": "The commentary develops rain as a mark on the earth and distinguishes people on the final path; Revelation seals God’s servants before harm comes to earth, sea, and trees."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b7ac37ffb55647601505", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.8.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bitter water brings death", "note": "The waters become bitter and people die from them. This is a specific negative counterpart to the commentary's sweet, abundant, life-sustaining water and to its image of water repairing rather than harming a community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b00b5928ce15849be85a", "ref": "Shepherd of Hermas, Vision 3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "tower founded on water", "note": "Hermas's vision of the Church as a tower built on water explains water as the means through which life is saved. This early Christian interpretation turns water into an actual foundation, closely echoing the commentary's qiwām and milāk language for what keeps a matter standing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76fa8a50dbd0b3bab39e", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 22a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Joseph's waterless pit interpreted", "note": "On Genesis 37:24, the Talmud reads \"no water\" as leaving room for snakes and scorpions. The interpretation sharpens the commentary's Joseph contrast: the biblical pit is not a usable water source even when it is not simply empty space."}
{"connection_id": "BC-913c09ca6fb04bfda838", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Ta'anit 23a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Honi's prayer for life-giving rain", "note": "The Honi story treats drought-ending rain as a communal need and distinguishes an unhelpful rain from the desired beneficial rain. It gives a Jewish narrative counterpart to the commentary's rain that repairs a people's condition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b5ea8fc2801e7dc69c84", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Ta'anit 7a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "water read as Torah", "note": "The Talmud develops the scriptural comparison of Torah with water. This changes water from material provision into descending and sought instruction, a useful interpretive bridge to the commentary's pairing of guidance with sustaining water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21521e4c94884e1e3a7d", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Ta'anit 9a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "clouds of glory and Miriam's well", "note": "The Talmud associates Israel's wilderness gifts with the cloud and Miriam's well. Its deliberate pairing is an especially close Jewish counterpart to this section's meeting of growing cloud and well."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8519e069ce6771defebc", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Berakhot 33a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer formula for wind and rain", "note": "The commentary joins wind, clouds, and rain; the Talmud explains the seasonal prayer formula that asks God to make wind blow and rain fall."}
{"connection_id": "BC-52f659496d45d481dc4d", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Ta'anit 2a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain and resurrection as divine acts", "note": "The commentary cites a Qur’anic scene where rain-bearing clouds lead into the raising of the dead; the Talmud groups rain and resurrection among powers God has not delegated, giving the same pair a Jewish theological frame."}
{"connection_id": "BC-664b3fb22532f83c247e", "ref": "Community Rule (1QS) III,13–IV,26", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two spirits and two ways", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the path of those favored from the paths of wrath and straying; the Community Rule contrasts the spirits of truth and perversity and their opposed ways, giving a Jewish sectarian account of moral paths."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6913497e530a7dbab60e", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 13:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Adam recognizes need of rain and prays", "note": "In the usual division, the midrash explains the delayed rain of Genesis 2 by saying that rain came after Adam recognized its need and prayed. It adds a Jewish reading in which rain, vegetation, human need, and prayer are deliberately joined."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d73bd5e93299859bc78", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 60:5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Rebekah's well water rises", "note": "The tradition commonly cited at this place has the well water rise for Rebekah as she draws for the camels. It re-reads the traveller-and-well service of Genesis 24 as a sign of blessing, adding a Jewish interpretive layer to the commentary's water as benefit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71b5ed5de2862160675b", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 70:8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jacob's well water rises", "note": "The common division of Genesis Rabbah says that the water rose to meet Jacob at Haran. That interpretive expansion transforms the covered well of Genesis 29 into a responsive source, useful beside the commentary's well apparatus and abundant water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-23d6306b800e3c27cd37", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 5:6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Miriam's well among primal gifts", "note": "The Mishnah lists Miriam's well among things prepared at creation's twilight. This later Jewish tradition makes a well a providential provision for Israel, clarifying the commentary's water as what sustains a travelling community."}

