{"connection_id": "BC-4bce01b6e35d37b91bfc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.1.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Mark repeats Isaiah’s straight-way formula", "note": "The commentary asks for guidance on a straight path; Mark opens John’s wilderness preaching with Isaiah’s call to prepare the Lord’s way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-35e1517867c88b76f9a3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.31", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the king judges the nations", "note": "The commentary names the Owner of the Day of Judgment and separates the favored from the rejected; Jesus depicts the Son of Man enthroned and gathering the nations for judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be8b31aaa8585638214a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.34", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessed inherit the kingdom", "note": "The commentary asks for the path of those granted favor rather than those under wrath; the king calls the blessed to inherit the kingdom prepared for them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ee88d3647f1d89fa98c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.41", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cursed depart from the king", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the favored from those under anger; the same judgment scene sends the cursed away, giving a concrete Christian counterpart to its divided outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-007ef9133c1107b8c624", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.3.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Matthew repeats Isaiah’s straight-way formula", "note": "The commentary asks for the straight path; Matthew applies Isaiah’s wilderness voice to John, who prepares the Lord’s way and makes his paths straight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff3a6c17d2c324069a20", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.5.45", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain falls on just and unjust", "note": "The commentary says rain corrects people’s condition and benefits them while the surah distinguishes favored from rejected paths; Jesus says God gives rain to both just and unjust, making rain’s reach broader than the moral division."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8343c0af0adc47ca577", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.5.45", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain from the heavenly Father", "note": "The Father sends rain on both just and unjust. It gives a New Testament articulation of rain as unearned divine beneficence, relevant to the commentary's rain as mercy and benefit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b838d340ad241453569", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kingdom and divine will", "note": "The commentary calls God master of the Judgment Day; the Lord’s Prayer petitions for God’s kingdom and will, placing divine rule before requests for provision and guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce38eac0fa3816694368", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "daily bread as provision", "note": "The commentary moves from rain and water to food for people and animals; the Lord’s Prayer asks for daily bread, making sustenance part of the prayer’s petition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b85d618fc44f65140e7e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lead away from trial and evil", "note": "The commentary asks for the right path and exclusion from straying; the Lord’s Prayer asks not to be led into trial and to be delivered from evil, a neighboring petition for safe direction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b47892c0450edadacc6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "name hallowed in prayer", "note": "The commentary opens with bism and develops its name/mark association; the Lord’s Prayer begins by asking that the Father’s name be hallowed, a parallel invocation before petitions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf6aaec0445a866f5091", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two roads with different ends", "note": "The commentary asks for the straight path and excludes the astray; Jesus contrasts the narrow gate and hard way leading to life with the broad way leading to destruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1404b1085a2b4c7ffa47", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "few find the life-giving way", "note": "The commentary asks to be guided on the straight road; Jesus says the way to life is narrow and difficult and few find it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-90c49170ca9c14d26a9d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.11.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shut sky so rain does not fall", "note": "The witnesses have authority to shut the sky so that rain does not fall during their prophecy. Its withheld-rain image is a specific inverse to the commentary's cloud and rain as mercy that restores life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05f2254739f59778b851", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "books opened for judgment", "note": "The commentary names the Day of Judgment; Revelation depicts the dead standing before the throne and being judged from the opened books according to their deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4d9b25d0f59faaf7dbf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.21.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water of life given freely", "note": "The thirsty receive water of life freely from the spring. It gives an eschatological form to the commentary's water as indispensable gift and remedy for need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0079ada1e8bd9472db55", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "river flows from the throne", "note": "The commentary calls water the support and “king” of human affairs; Revelation’s river of life flows from the throne of God and the Lamb, joining rule to life-giving water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59d29482ed5b4197c542", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "river of water of life", "note": "A bright river of water of life proceeds from God's throne. The throne-source relationship gives a Christian counterpart to the commentary's unusual claim that water is a kingdom's or traveller's holding power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c200fdae91dbc30ce7ab", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree and water sustain life", "note": "The commentary links gathered water to persistent vegetation; Revelation places the tree of life beside the river, bearing fruit and leaves for healing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db2b429835b1d8509289", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruit trees and healing leaves", "note": "The trees beside the life-river bear fruit continually and their leaves serve healing. Its flourishing trees supply a Christian counterpart to the commentary's plant nourished into unfading freshness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0e5759ebc635fdd0199", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide to springs of living water", "note": "The commentary joins guidance on a road to water for the traveler; the Lamb shepherds the redeemed and guides them to springs of living water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c7910c470a5270054fc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guidance to springs of life-water", "note": "The Lamb shepherds and guides the thirsty to springs of the water of life. It closely meets the commentary's way, guidance, shepherding provision, and water that removes want."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a63fb5a1aba4cfa5dec5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seal protects servants before harm", "note": "The commentary develops rain as a mark on the earth and distinguishes people on the final path; Revelation seals God’s servants before harm comes to earth, sea, and trees."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b7ac37ffb55647601505", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.8.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bitter water brings death", "note": "The waters become bitter and people die from them. This is a specific negative counterpart to the commentary's sweet, abundant, life-sustaining water and to its image of water repairing rather than harming a community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b00b5928ce15849be85a", "ref": "Shepherd of Hermas, Vision 3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "tower founded on water", "note": "Hermas's vision of the Church as a tower built on water explains water as the means through which life is saved. This early Christian interpretation turns water into an actual foundation, closely echoing the commentary's qiwām and milāk language for what keeps a matter standing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76fa8a50dbd0b3bab39e", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 22a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Joseph's waterless pit interpreted", "note": "On Genesis 37:24, the Talmud reads \"no water\" as leaving room for snakes and scorpions. The interpretation sharpens the commentary's Joseph contrast: the biblical pit is not a usable water source even when it is not simply empty space."}
{"connection_id": "BC-913c09ca6fb04bfda838", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Ta'anit 23a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Honi's prayer for life-giving rain", "note": "The Honi story treats drought-ending rain as a communal need and distinguishes an unhelpful rain from the desired beneficial rain. It gives a Jewish narrative counterpart to the commentary's rain that repairs a people's condition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b5ea8fc2801e7dc69c84", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Ta'anit 7a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "water read as Torah", "note": "The Talmud develops the scriptural comparison of Torah with water. This changes water from material provision into descending and sought instruction, a useful interpretive bridge to the commentary's pairing of guidance with sustaining water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21521e4c94884e1e3a7d", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Ta'anit 9a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "clouds of glory and Miriam's well", "note": "The Talmud associates Israel's wilderness gifts with the cloud and Miriam's well. Its deliberate pairing is an especially close Jewish counterpart to this section's meeting of growing cloud and well."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8519e069ce6771defebc", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Berakhot 33a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer formula for wind and rain", "note": "The commentary joins wind, clouds, and rain; the Talmud explains the seasonal prayer formula that asks God to make wind blow and rain fall."}
{"connection_id": "BC-52f659496d45d481dc4d", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Ta'anit 2a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain and resurrection as divine acts", "note": "The commentary cites a Qur’anic scene where rain-bearing clouds lead into the raising of the dead; the Talmud groups rain and resurrection among powers God has not delegated, giving the same pair a Jewish theological frame."}
{"connection_id": "BC-664b3fb22532f83c247e", "ref": "Community Rule (1QS) III,13–IV,26", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two spirits and two ways", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the path of those favored from the paths of wrath and straying; the Community Rule contrasts the spirits of truth and perversity and their opposed ways, giving a Jewish sectarian account of moral paths."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6913497e530a7dbab60e", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 13:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Adam recognizes need of rain and prays", "note": "In the usual division, the midrash explains the delayed rain of Genesis 2 by saying that rain came after Adam recognized its need and prayed. It adds a Jewish reading in which rain, vegetation, human need, and prayer are deliberately joined."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d73bd5e93299859bc78", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 60:5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Rebekah's well water rises", "note": "The tradition commonly cited at this place has the well water rise for Rebekah as she draws for the camels. It re-reads the traveller-and-well service of Genesis 24 as a sign of blessing, adding a Jewish interpretive layer to the commentary's water as benefit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71b5ed5de2862160675b", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 70:8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jacob's well water rises", "note": "The common division of Genesis Rabbah says that the water rose to meet Jacob at Haran. That interpretive expansion transforms the covered well of Genesis 29 into a responsive source, useful beside the commentary's well apparatus and abundant water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-23d6306b800e3c27cd37", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 5:6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Miriam's well among primal gifts", "note": "The Mishnah lists Miriam's well among things prepared at creation's twilight. This later Jewish tradition makes a well a providential provision for Israel, clarifying the commentary's water as what sustains a travelling community."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-22d9fc7b596393e8a2bd", "ref": "Mishnah Eruvin 2:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "well enclosures for pilgrims and animals", "note": "The Mishnah permits a distinctive post-and-enclosure arrangement around wells so that festival travellers' animals can be watered. Its legal concern with well-side construction, travellers, and animal water is unusually close to the commentary's detailed well-mouth apparatus."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6616e38bb15fb83c9e62", "ref": "Mishnah Ta'anit 1:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seasonal request for rain", "note": "The Mishnah fixes when a community begins mentioning and requesting rain. Its concrete liturgical ordering of rain gives a Jewish communal counterpart to the commentary's rain as mercy and repair rather than merely weather."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c1e3113a34c904015e2", "ref": "Mishnah, Rosh Hashanah 1:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the world judged concerning water", "note": "The commentary places rain, harvest, and the Day of Judgment together; the Mishnah says the world is judged at Sukkot concerning water, joining seasonal rain to a global judgment calendar."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d44090bc041f303a8ba", "ref": "Mishnah, Sukkah 4:9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water drawing as a temple rite", "note": "The commentary describes water drawn from a well and rain as divine provision; the Mishnah describes drawing water from Siloam for the Temple’s water libation, making water-drawing a liturgical act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14d4ede005007f2bda58", "ref": "Mishnah, Ta'anit 1:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seasonal mention of rain in prayer", "note": "The commentary develops rain and wind as divine provision; the Mishnah debates when the rain petition enters the daily prayer cycle, showing rain as a recurring Jewish liturgical request."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd20a3d0e829970acc76", "ref": "Sirach 39:26", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water among life’s necessities", "note": "The commentary calls water the support of affairs and traces it into food; Sirach lists water among the necessities of human life beside bread, fire, salt, and other provisions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0cdf0ff3f81f5cbe486", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.44", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a small cloud announces rain", "note": "The commentary follows wind-borne clouds until they become heavy with rain; Elijah’s servant sees a small cloud rising from the sea as the sign of coming rain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba69d74cb6c032068dfa", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.44", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "small cloud rises from sea", "note": "Elijah's servant sees a small cloud rise from the sea before the rain. It is a focused companion to the commentary's cloud that forms above and becomes the bearer of growing water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-271ca1a0ef648a0f9d31", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.45", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wind, cloud, and heavy rain", "note": "The commentary makes wind and cloud the forerunners of rain; in Elijah’s scene the sky darkens with clouds and wind before a great rain falls."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc3cc28df31bd6ad42d3", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.45", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "clouds, wind, and heavy rain", "note": "The sky turns dark with clouds and wind before a great rain. Its linked weather actors answer the commentary's prolonged cloud and moist south-wind scene without requiring the same wind direction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93645b1cc470988a076c", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.2.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "healing bad water and barrenness", "note": "Elisha throws salt into a spring and declares its waters healed, so that death and barrenness will no longer come from it. It gives a sharp biblical counterpart to rain or water improving a people's condition and to the commentary's fresh, beneficial water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-155907ffd459175de62b", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.3.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water without wind or rain", "note": "The valley will be filled with water even though neither wind nor rain is seen. Its explicit separation of water provision from visible wind and rain is a useful contrary configuration beside the commentary's wind moving cloud toward rain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05c218715be61bbd97e4", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a king longs for well water", "note": "The commentary says a tribe calls its waters “our kings” because water holds affairs together; David, himself king, longs for a drink from the well of Bethlehem."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fdd89ca83ecf6de7653a", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "king longs for gate-well water", "note": "David longs for water from the well by Bethlehem's gate. The king's desire for a particular well makes water a royal concern, a concrete biblical companion to the commentary's phrase that a community's waters are its kings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3468a3b2e5b473300f1", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "men risk their lives to draw water", "note": "The commentary says the traveler’s water lets him hold his affairs in hand; David’s men break through enemy lines to draw the well water he longs for."}
{"connection_id": "BC-974148d01c4c0df03e38", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "men draw and carry well water to David", "note": "David's warriors break through, draw water from the Bethlehem well, and bring it to him. The verse supplies the physical act of drawing and carrying water for a ruler, beside the commentary's pulley, traveller's water, and royal-water wordplay."}
{"connection_id": "BC-426b06c01b5cb8096cf6", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water valued as life itself", "note": "The commentary treats water as the support of life and rule; David refuses to drink the water because it represents the blood of men who risked their lives to fetch it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6feabd89e0f0f9b8b06c", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "court sits and books open", "note": "The commentary names the Day of Judgment; Daniel sees the court sit and books open, giving an apocalyptic court scene for divine judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55b052af4a44c5e30614", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a human-like figure approaches the Ancient of Days", "note": "The commentary calls God the sovereign of the judgment day; Daniel sees one like a son of man come before the Ancient of Days in the heavenly court."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d058cde9c23bbaeb7ee", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "everlasting dominion over peoples", "note": "The commentary calls God mālik and Lord of all worlds; Daniel’s human-like figure receives dominion and a kingdom served by all peoples and nations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-206fd50b8bec0b549588", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "land drinks rain from heaven", "note": "The commentary follows rain into soil and plants; Deuteronomy contrasts a land that drinks rain from heaven with Egypt’s irrigation, making rainfall the land’s source of fertility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-072e785714f40ce258fd", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "land drinks rain from heaven", "note": "The promised land drinks water from rain of the heavens, directly holding together sky, rain, ground, and dependence that the commentary develops under the Arabic sky/cloud naming."}
{"connection_id": "BC-159e3c0f66908d2b8d8b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic samāʾ and Hebrew šāmayim", "note": "The commentary's source label س م و develops sky, cloud, rain, and plant under samāʾ. Hebrew šāmayim in this verse is a cognate sky term, but the verse keeps heaven and its rain distinct while making the land drink the rain; that difference is informative rather than evidence for identical usage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b94c73f72f45bab358f8", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain yields food crops", "note": "The commentary traces rain to vegetation and provision; Deuteronomy promises rain at its proper season so grain, wine, and oil can grow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40c211292d5dfc55b505", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain, harvest, grass for animals", "note": "The rain in its season yields grain, wine, oil, and grass for livestock. This is a close biblical analogue to the commentary's sequence from poured water to human food, animal provision, and livelihood."}
{"connection_id": "BC-022094dec8da32f457fb", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.26", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessing or curse set before the people", "note": "The commentary distinguishes those granted favor from those under wrath or astray; Deuteronomy explicitly sets blessing and curse before Israel in the same chapter that ties rain to obedience and harvest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0207797ccfa68ba73860", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.27", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "obedience brings blessing", "note": "The commentary asks for the path of those given favor; Deuteronomy says blessing follows listening to the LORD’s commandments."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e58944456af5012da6c5", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.28", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turning aside brings curse", "note": "The commentary asks not to follow the straying path; Deuteronomy names a curse for disobeying and turning aside to other gods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-017453b7b21dd88c26eb", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life and good set against death and evil", "note": "The commentary asks for the straight path and distinguishes its people from those who stray; Moses places life and good before Israel alongside death and evil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5666fedad1e54ce3d189", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choose life rather than death", "note": "The commentary seeks the way of those granted favor, not the way of wrath; Moses calls heaven and earth as witnesses and sets life and death, blessing and curse before the people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-456efbed8559078c436e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.20", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hold fast to God, who is life", "note": "The commentary asks to be guided on a path that sustains life; Deuteronomy says to love the LORD, listen to his voice, and hold fast to him because he is the people’s life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-084fce5ac49baa4db152", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teaching falls like rain", "note": "The commentary says rain writes its mark in green growth and asks for guidance; Moses compares his teaching to rain and dew that fall on tender grass, making the same image a vehicle for instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c035e107a0f741404f78", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "possible r-b-b cognate in revivim", "note": "The commentary labels its cloud and tender-plant forms from R-B-B; Deuteronomy uses Hebrew revivim for showers falling on tender grass, a plausible cognate cluster whose exact lexical relationship should be checked in the Hebrew text."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30f90efca0557273700b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "springs and streams in good land", "note": "The commentary pairs the traveler’s road with water and treats water as the support of livelihood; Deuteronomy describes a land of brooks, springs, and deep waters that sustains its inhabitants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f70fa9b261cbb38d5f8a", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brooks, springs, and underground waters", "note": "The good land is defined by brooks, fountains, and depths issuing in valley and hill. It complements the commentary's deep water-filled well and its concern that a water source might withdraw underground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef43b50c50bd574f1424", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "full clouds empty rain on earth", "note": "When clouds are full, they pour rain on the earth. The compact image answers the commentary's layered cloud, its accumulated moisture, and the eventual transfer of gathered water to the ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e41baf8679c59fda605", "ref": "WLC:Exod.15.25", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bitter water made sweet", "note": "The waters at Marah are made sweet so the people can drink. It offers a specific biblical analogue to the commentary's distinction of abundant water that can be called sweet and water that repairs a people's condition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-63bc3c4e214e5fc39225", "ref": "WLC:Exod.15.27", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "oasis springs, palms, and encampment", "note": "Israel reaches Elim, with twelve water springs and seventy palms, and camps by the water. This joins a travelling people, a water source, and flourishing vegetation in the concrete way the commentary joins road, well, and growing ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d62f1f443a7cd04d5b3", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thirst during a wilderness journey", "note": "The commentary links the road with the traveler’s need for water; Exodus says Israel travels through the wilderness and has no water to drink, making thirst an immediate danger of the route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b64023a9d71bd76472e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water from the rock", "note": "The commentary treats water as what keeps a traveler’s affairs standing; in the wilderness God brings water from the rock at Horeb for the people to drink."}

