{"connection_id": "BC-6616e38bb15fb83c9e62", "ref": "Mishnah Ta'anit 1:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seasonal request for rain", "note": "The Mishnah fixes when a community begins mentioning and requesting rain. Its concrete liturgical ordering of rain gives a Jewish communal counterpart to the commentary's rain as mercy and repair rather than merely weather."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c1e3113a34c904015e2", "ref": "Mishnah, Rosh Hashanah 1:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the world judged concerning water", "note": "The commentary places rain, harvest, and the Day of Judgment together; the Mishnah says the world is judged at Sukkot concerning water, joining seasonal rain to a global judgment calendar."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d44090bc041f303a8ba", "ref": "Mishnah, Sukkah 4:9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water drawing as a temple rite", "note": "The commentary describes water drawn from a well and rain as divine provision; the Mishnah describes drawing water from Siloam for the Temple’s water libation, making water-drawing a liturgical act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14d4ede005007f2bda58", "ref": "Mishnah, Ta'anit 1:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seasonal mention of rain in prayer", "note": "The commentary develops rain and wind as divine provision; the Mishnah debates when the rain petition enters the daily prayer cycle, showing rain as a recurring Jewish liturgical request."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd20a3d0e829970acc76", "ref": "Sirach 39:26", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water among life’s necessities", "note": "The commentary calls water the support of affairs and traces it into food; Sirach lists water among the necessities of human life beside bread, fire, salt, and other provisions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0cdf0ff3f81f5cbe486", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.44", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a small cloud announces rain", "note": "The commentary follows wind-borne clouds until they become heavy with rain; Elijah’s servant sees a small cloud rising from the sea as the sign of coming rain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba69d74cb6c032068dfa", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.44", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "small cloud rises from sea", "note": "Elijah's servant sees a small cloud rise from the sea before the rain. It is a focused companion to the commentary's cloud that forms above and becomes the bearer of growing water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-271ca1a0ef648a0f9d31", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.45", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wind, cloud, and heavy rain", "note": "The commentary makes wind and cloud the forerunners of rain; in Elijah’s scene the sky darkens with clouds and wind before a great rain falls."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc3cc28df31bd6ad42d3", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.45", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "clouds, wind, and heavy rain", "note": "The sky turns dark with clouds and wind before a great rain. Its linked weather actors answer the commentary's prolonged cloud and moist south-wind scene without requiring the same wind direction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93645b1cc470988a076c", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.2.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "healing bad water and barrenness", "note": "Elisha throws salt into a spring and declares its waters healed, so that death and barrenness will no longer come from it. It gives a sharp biblical counterpart to rain or water improving a people's condition and to the commentary's fresh, beneficial water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-155907ffd459175de62b", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.3.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water without wind or rain", "note": "The valley will be filled with water even though neither wind nor rain is seen. Its explicit separation of water provision from visible wind and rain is a useful contrary configuration beside the commentary's wind moving cloud toward rain."}
