{"connection_id": "BC-94bab32903622060d47d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.29.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "waiting to water the flocks", "note": "The commentary says the women wait to water until shepherds depart; Genesis has shepherds wait until all flocks gather before moving the stone and watering them, making control of access to a well part of the scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb5d1ba47dc5384f155e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.22", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "brothers plan a pit rescue", "note": "The commentary says Yusuf’s brothers propose the pit so a passing traveler may take him; Reuben instead proposes the pit to keep the brothers from killing Joseph, a shared rescue-through-the-pit plan with a changed rationale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11b5bf827bfd7e81b3e7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Joseph in the pit", "note": "The commentary describes Yusuf thrown into the dark bottom of a well before travelers arrive; Genesis has Joseph put into an empty cistern, matching the pit while withholding the Qur’anic water-drawing detail."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d918e31e847b28415c5e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "empty cistern versus water drawing", "note": "The commentary’s next Yusuf scene has a bucket lowered to draw water and a boy discovered; Genesis explicitly says Joseph’s cistern was empty, making the means of discovery differ."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e09886bd6968d23960ed", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Joseph's pit has no water", "note": "Genesis explicitly calls Joseph's pit empty and waterless. That sharpens the commentary's contrasting Qur'anic well scene, where a caravan's water-drawer lowers a bucket and finds the boy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70df998f667fda27bdd0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.28", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "caravan and the boy in the pit", "note": "The commentary says a caravan arrives and its water drawer finds Yusuf; Genesis has traders pass and Joseph brought up from the pit and sold to the Ishmaelites, sharing the pit-and-caravan turn."}
{"connection_id": "BC-94d71d61c243df05333e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.28", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "who brings Joseph out", "note": "The commentary makes the lowered water bucket the way the passing caravan finds Yusuf; Genesis has traders pull Joseph from the pit and sell him, replacing a chance discovery during water-drawing with deliberate removal and sale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1bd48e656449b1df46b9", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.28", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "travellers draw Joseph from pit", "note": "Midianite traders draw and lift Joseph from the pit and carry him away. The commentary's Qur'anic caravan likewise removes Joseph from a well, but gives the discovery to a water-carrier lowering his bucket."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16f1cee162ceef2dbb11", "ref": "WLC:Hos.14.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dew makes Israel blossom", "note": "God will be like dew to Israel, and Israel will blossom like the lily. This adds the secondary, non-rain moisture route by which divine beneficence makes a plant fresh and flourishing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17fc0bb7889403a9628a", "ref": "WLC:Hos.6.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "revival on the third day", "note": "The commentary cites rain as a sign of the earth’s revival and connects rain to raising the dead; Hosea promises that God will revive Israel and raise it up on the third day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3bc67fdcfa5511845c66", "ref": "WLC:Hos.6.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God comes like seasonal rain", "note": "The commentary makes rain a sign of divine benefit and renewal; Hosea compares God’s coming to dawn and to rain that waters the earth, giving the rain a covenantal and restorative meaning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-19699a7b2cc5c551d87d", "ref": "WLC:Hos.6.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God comes as latter rain", "note": "God's coming is compared to rain and to the latter rain watering the earth. It directly supplies the rain-as-divine-mercy reading that the commentary develops around rahmet and revived ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc9697a56ad35b18f5e8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.12.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "drawing from wells of salvation", "note": "Joyful drawing from wells becomes the image of salvation. The verse gives a scriptural way in which the literal action central to the commentary's pulley and well bears a second, saving sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4812df3fef40b49628f6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.27.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God continually waters vineyard", "note": "YHWH guards the vineyard and waters it continually. It supplies the direct divine-cultivator sense that the commentary hears in Rab as the one associated with plant growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8bdd6db9477b9268c71", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "“this is the way”", "note": "The commentary asks God to guide people on a straight road; Isaiah promises a voice behind the listener saying “this is the way, walk in it,” an explicit divine direction for the traveler."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c81330f9ddd6beee624d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain for the seed", "note": "The commentary joins the straight road to rain-fed growth and food; immediately after Isaiah’s way instruction, God sends rain for the seed sown in the ground and makes the harvest abundant."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4aed2cc8881a4c1f1d0d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain for seed, rich bread, livestock", "note": "Rain is given for the seed sown in the ground, producing abundant bread and pasture for livestock. This directly meets the commentary's water, food, animals, and sustaining livelihood."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d8c053e92aea3fe606e0", "ref": "WLC:Isa.32.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wind shelter, dry-land stream, and shade", "note": "The commentary brings wind, water, and Musa’s shade together; Isaiah describes a refuge from wind and storm, streams in dry land, and shade from a great rock in a weary land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce1df354eae5cf5802e5", "ref": "WLC:Isa.32.20", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sowing by waters with ox and donkey", "note": "The blessed sow beside all waters and let ox and donkey range out. It joins water, cultivation, and working animals, the same practical provision sequence that the commentary traces from rain to food and livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e284c4160387ef293aa", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water in the wilderness", "note": "The commentary places road travel, thirst, and water together; Isaiah says waters break out in the wilderness and streams in the desert, turning the barren route into a place of life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f8b0c24b7867be30809", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "waters in wilderness, streams in desert", "note": "Waters break out in the wilderness and streams in the desert. The verse makes the life-restoring turn from dryness to water that the commentary associates with rain, mercy, and fresh life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e42a74b0c5e5aae885c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "highway through the redeemed land", "note": "The commentary’s sirat is a path the traveler walks with water at hand; Isaiah 35:8 describes a highway called the Holy Way for the redeemed, pairing a marked route with the wilderness water of the same restoration scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09e76651d63d30393e1e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight highway in the wilderness", "note": "The commentary pairs the straight path with travel and water in the wilderness; Isaiah calls for a highway to be made straight in the desert for God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc48d745de7549beeddb", "ref": "WLC:Isa.41.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "needy seek water and find none", "note": "The poor and needy seek water with their tongues parched, and God answers them. It is a direct companion to the commentary's question of who can bring water when a community's water has gone down."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92f13e9c221af72fa70c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.41.18", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rivers, fountains, pools on dry land", "note": "God opens rivers and fountains and turns wilderness into pools and dry ground into springs. This is a direct biblical counterpart to both the commentary's well-water and its fear that water may sink out of reach."}
{"connection_id": "BC-03ffdb666d453905c742", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water poured on thirsty ground", "note": "The commentary’s rain repairs people’s condition and benefits them; Isaiah promises water for thirsty land and streams on dry ground, followed by God’s spirit on the people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0934854deb7584016aba", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water poured on thirsty land", "note": "Water is poured on thirsty land and streams on dry ground. It directly parallels the commentary's rain sent to barren earth, while its following spiritual application makes the literal image more than a weather report."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac3fe7d08e04992be6bf", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "growth where water arrives", "note": "The commentary says the first rain marks earth with plants; Isaiah pictures descendants springing up like grass and willows beside flowing water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c988613b8ec089ebb522", "ref": "WLC:Isa.45.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "heavens rain, earth sprouts salvation", "note": "The heavens rain and the earth opens to sprout salvation and righteousness. It recasts the commentary's rain-and-growing-earth image as an ethical and saving figure rather than a purely agricultural one."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28e5956e094d0acaff62", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God guides people to springs", "note": "The commentary’s straight road includes water for the traveler; Isaiah says the compassionate guide leads the redeemed beside springs, where they do not hunger or thirst."}
{"connection_id": "BC-443914a5086443f569cc", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guidance to springs of water", "note": "The compassionate guide leads the hungry and thirsty to springs of water. Its explicit joining of guidance, a way, and water is a strong companion to the commentary's straight path beside the sustaining well."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eff7d34048e5055f78d4", "ref": "WLC:Isa.53.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sheep gone astray from their way", "note": "The commentary asks not to follow those who stray and elsewhere describes flocks at the well; Isaiah says all sheep have gone astray, each turning to its own way, joining animal wandering to the path language."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c04a52a75202a540fe9", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thirsty invited to water", "note": "The thirsty are invited to come to water without payment. Its invitation turns the commentary's traveller's necessary water into a public gift available to those in need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e0360d1594044f2526c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain produces seed and bread", "note": "The commentary’s first rain marks soil with vegetation and then becomes food; Isaiah says rain and snow water the earth, make it bud, and provide seed and bread."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7080d3abf5cff3cac2e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain’s work and the effective word", "note": "The commentary describes rain as a mark written into the earth and asks for guidance; Isaiah compares the word from God’s mouth to rain that does not return before accomplishing its purpose."}
{"connection_id": "BC-62b3ba5df48d562ee0dd", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guidance and a watered garden", "note": "The commentary joins a request for the straight path to water that sustains life; Isaiah says the LORD will guide continually and make the faithful like a watered garden and an unfailing spring."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a390167eb9d75d182c50", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "continual guidance and watered garden", "note": "God continually guides, satisfies the parched person, and makes that person like a watered garden and an unfailing spring. It closely joins the commentary's guidance, fresh life, and never-failing water."}
