{"connection_id": "BC-f72ae3a4cbb53f41535c", "ref": "WLC:Job.37.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "moisture loads thick cloud", "note": "The thick cloud is loaded with moisture and then spread abroad. Its particular image of a moisture-bearing cloud parallels the commentary's layered cloud and the wet wind associated with it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9fbef680abdb388adb4a", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.26", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain for empty wilderness", "note": "The commentary presents rain as what restores soil and makes it useful; Job asks who sends rain on land where no person lives, watering the wilderness for growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d59a42e5d6ab0d8f7f1d", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "early and late rain", "note": "The commentary follows rain into green growth and provision; Joel promises early and late rains as part of restoration, tying weather to renewed harvest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d926c14a1060b85d2fb4", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "early and latter rain", "note": "The rain is given in its proper season, including the early and latter rains. It provides a biblical seasonal setting for the commentary's first rain that marks the earth with vegetation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-51895513c089fffe8697", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grain and vats after rain", "note": "The commentary traces water toward food; Joel’s restoration scene has threshing floors full of grain and vats overflowing after the rains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef87dc08b4a8f61b6a00", "ref": "WLC:Lev.26.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain in its season", "note": "The commentary’s cloud and rain enlarge the earth with plant growth; Leviticus promises seasonal rain followed by a land that yields produce and trees that bear fruit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4781f9a682060f05d60d", "ref": "WLC:Lev.26.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seasonal rain, yield, fruit", "note": "Seasonal rain makes the land yield produce and trees bear fruit. It directly supplies the rain-to-growing-earth sequence through which the commentary hears the growing Rab and the rain-marked ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-481940bd84fa2dcae776", "ref": "WLC:Num.20.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water gushes from the rock again", "note": "The commentary presents water as life-sustaining provision; in a second wilderness account Moses strikes the rock and water gushes out for the congregation and their animals."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef75e9cc1983ad5ea827", "ref": "WLC:Num.20.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "king's highway and wells", "note": "Israel asks to travel on the king's highway while promising not to drink from field, vineyard, or well. The verse unusually binds road, traveller, well water, and king in one practical scene, directly meeting the commentary's straight path and water-as-royal-support imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b455c8467a0d8ea3b75d", "ref": "WLC:Num.21.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gives water at Beer", "note": "The commentary joins journey, well, and water for people and flocks; at Beer God tells Moses to gather the people and promises to give them water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-930197b4bb00304d9840", "ref": "WLC:Num.21.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gathered people given water", "note": "At Beer, God commands that the people be gathered so that he may give them water. The collective provisioning clarifies why the commentary treats a well as the support of an affair rather than a private possession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1e78b3fbcd8b9094145d", "ref": "WLC:Num.21.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "song to the well", "note": "The commentary carefully develops the well’s mouth and the apparatus for drawing water; Israel sings to the well, “Spring up,” making a well itself the focus of a public water song."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b77b4e001ad743662b78", "ref": "WLC:Num.21.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "song to the well", "note": "Israel answers the provision of water with the command to the well to spring up and with a song. The liturgical address makes the well itself a source of communal life, matching the commentary's concentrated water imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-524a90bdc9200d2d1027", "ref": "WLC:Num.21.18", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "princes dig the well", "note": "The commentary says water is the support and “king” of communal affairs; Numbers says princes and nobles dug this well with their staffs, tying water access to leaders’ work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8d472914d9c48f10080", "ref": "WLC:Num.21.18", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "well dug by nobles with staffs", "note": "The well is dug by princes and hollowed by nobles with their staffs. As the necessary continuation of the well-song, it makes rulership and a constructed water source meet, closely accompanying the commentary's waters called rulers and its beam-and-pulley well mouth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c82949ddab48fb9ecff1", "ref": "WLC:Num.6.27", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew shem, name placed on people", "note": "The commentary labels bism with a name-related source and then links it to wasm, a mark made by first rain; Numbers says the priests put God’s name upon Israel and God blesses them, joining name, marking, and benefit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c882a28d3778ed470298", "ref": "WLC:Prov.25.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud and wind without rain", "note": "A person who boasts of a false gift is like clouds and wind with no rain. This negative weather image precisely defines the loss implicit in the commentary's productive cloud: visible cloud and wind do not suffice unless water reaches the earth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4518095a4132e63e6974", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.20", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deep sources and dripping skies", "note": "By divine knowledge the depths break open and the skies drip dew. It is a compact biblical pairing of water from below with moisture from above, closely matching the commentary's well-water and heaven-rain as two routes of water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-46b7387474a442c548fe", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God makes the path straight", "note": "The commentary’s central request is guidance on the straight path; Proverbs says that acknowledging God in all one’s ways results in God making the paths straight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0248261a7ae937aceeea", "ref": "WLC:Prov.5.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cistern and own well as ethical figure", "note": "The proverb commands drinking from one's own cistern and well. It takes the literal infrastructure central to the commentary and makes its contained water an ethical image of faithful, rightly bounded household life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e2caecc0566fc29138b6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree beside streams, leaf does not wither", "note": "The commentary’s ribba is a tender plant said not to wither in summer; Psalm 1’s tree by streams bears fruit and keeps its leaf, joining steady water to enduring growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d2310e26f0345d69e11", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree by water, leaf does not wither", "note": "The tree planted by channels of water bears fruit and does not wither. It is the closest biblical image for the commentary's tender plant that remains fresh even through summer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92f7fd2ade9bfb8c53fd", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "standing at judgment", "note": "The commentary pairs the straight path with the Day of Judgment and distinguishes graced people from those under wrath; Psalm 1 says the wicked will not stand in judgment or in the righteous assembly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce2cbca1990a8a2bd8f7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew qum, stand", "note": "The commentary labels mustaqim from Q-W-M and says water is what makes affairs stand; Psalm 1 uses Hebrew yaqumu for sinners who will not stand in judgment, connecting uprightness with the judgment scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8299bb0ad0e93a7fd702", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the righteous way and the perishing way", "note": "The commentary asks for the path of the favored rather than those who stray; Psalm 1 says the LORD knows the way of the righteous while the wicked way perishes."}

