{"connection_id": "BC-d6e2bbd38aa974a7d931", "ref": "WLC:Ps.25.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "appeal to remembered mercy", "note": "The commentary grounds guidance in divine favor and benefit; the psalm asks God to remember compassion and steadfast love, linking direction to mercy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca329ca9ab765bbd9cf1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.29.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew melekh over flood", "note": "The verse calls YHWH melekh, a cognate of the commentary's م ل ك / mālik label, while placing him over the flood. It gives a substantive Hebrew king-and-water meeting, not merely a shared root, beside the commentary's water as what holds an affair together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-44a3d93f8b0d501e093b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.46.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "river makes the city unshakable", "note": "The commentary says water is the support by which affairs stand; Psalm 46’s river gladdens God’s city, where God’s presence keeps it from being moved."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc3f5d31feccf40e7afb", "ref": "WLC:Ps.47.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew melek, king", "note": "The commentary labels Qur’anic mālik from M-L-K and calls water “our kings”; Psalm 47 uses the cognate Hebrew melek for God as king over all earth, showing the sovereignty sense beyond the water metaphor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aac06c0000adbaba83ba", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God visits and waters earth", "note": "The commentary links rain with mercy and the revival of soil; Psalm 65 says God visits and waters the earth, filling the divine river and preparing grain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-570de5601e308db14d02", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "furrows watered, growth blessed", "note": "God waters furrows, softens earth with showers, and blesses its growth. This particularly matches the commentary's first rain writing vegetation into the ground and the cloud that grows plants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ce981f9a535f29c2ebc", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "showers soften furrows and bless growth", "note": "The commentary traces the first rain into green plants and provision; Psalm 65 pictures showers softening the furrows and blessing the growth of the earth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f881d8c5e30bc7e3442e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God’s tracks drip abundance", "note": "The commentary says rain leaves a green mark on the soil and pairs water with the straight path; Psalm 65 says God’s tracks drip with abundance as the year is crowned with goodness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-68900e387d3116051b22", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pastures and valleys yield", "note": "The commentary takes rain through soil to plants and food; Psalm 65 ends with pastures, hills, and valleys clothed in flocks and grain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-60ef789adfcfd340e490", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praise before the rain hymn", "note": "The commentary cites rain, mercy, and hamd together in Qur’an 42:28; Psalm 65 opens with praise to God and then describes God watering the earth and making it fruitful."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c530dffe5047fdb4573", "ref": "WLC:Ps.67.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grace and blessing for the people", "note": "The commentary connects hamd with rain and mercy; Psalm 67 asks God to be gracious and bless the people before describing the earth’s yield and the nations’ praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e9396f946573d185f392", "ref": "WLC:Ps.67.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earth yields its increase", "note": "The commentary traces rain to plants and food; Psalm 67 says the earth has yielded its increase and asks God to bless the people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-07423c0423c888fb93fc", "ref": "WLC:Ps.67.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all nations fear God", "note": "The commentary names the Lord of all worlds and invokes praise; Psalm 67 ends with all the ends of the earth fearing God after the earth’s harvest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55236aee3dae5a2b7458", "ref": "WLC:Ps.72.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the king judges with justice", "note": "The commentary calls God mālik on the Day of Judgment and says water is “our king”; Psalm 72 asks that the king judge the people with righteousness and the poor with justice."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-d1dc0cbda60f5b7f9c26", "ref": "WLC:Ps.72.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain on cut grass under the king", "note": "The commentary joins the kingly water image to rain-fed growth; Psalm 72 compares the king’s reign to rain falling on mown grass and showers watering the earth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-07c14fb1418cc11f991c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.78.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water from the deep in the wilderness", "note": "The commentary explains that water holds up the traveler’s affairs; Psalm 78 recalls God splitting rocks in the wilderness and giving drink as from the deep."}
{"connection_id": "BC-03b96f28fcc3e3f6ac3b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.78.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "streams from a rock", "note": "The commentary moves between the deep well and water that sustains life; Psalm 78 says streams came from the rock and ran like rivers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb284f5200b214cf65f4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.78.20", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water and food on the journey", "note": "The commentary traces water toward food and livelihood; Psalm 78 recalls water gushing from the rock and immediately asks whether God can also provide bread and meat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4a3875f3534b10d0990", "ref": "WLC:Ps.84.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Baca valley made a spring by rain", "note": "Those crossing the Valley of Baca make it a place of springs, and early rain covers it with blessings. The transforming rain-and-spring image supplies a pilgrimage-scale companion to the commentary's path that cannot be separated from water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-00257af951f1a52ad713", "ref": "WLC:Ps.96.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God reigns over the world", "note": "The commentary names God as ruler of the Day of Judgment and Lord of the worlds; Psalm 96 says the LORD reigns and judges the peoples with equity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9862dc0b3109dc9a7c8f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.96.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God comes to judge the earth", "note": "The commentary calls God the Owner of the Day of Judgment; Psalm 96 says God comes to judge the earth with righteousness and the peoples with faithfulness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8bbdb8f760cd7198852f", "ref": "WLC:Song.4.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "garden fountain and well of living water", "note": "The beloved is called a garden fountain, a well of living waters, and streams flowing from Lebanon. Its gathered garden, well, freshness, and flow make it a particularly close biblical companion to the commentary's full well and unfading vegetation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93b534e5fbecbe8ed295", "ref": "WLC:Zech.10.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ask for rain in its season", "note": "The commentary’s imagery treats rain as provision from God; Zechariah explicitly tells people to ask the LORD for rain at the time of spring rain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eacd16f34eb423893f5d", "ref": "WLC:Zech.10.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "spring rain, storm clouds, field plants", "note": "God makes storm clouds, gives rain showers, and provides vegetation in every field. The verse has the same cloud-rain-plant sequence developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a422d4b11fd6e529316", "ref": "WLC:Zech.14.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "living waters in summer and winter", "note": "Living waters flow in both summer and winter. Its year-round flow is a close counterpart to the commentary's fresh plant that does not fade in summer and its well of plentiful water."}

